{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Elohim finished</b>, etc. The plain sense of the verse is that it comes to teach us not to suppose that He performed any other act of creation after this that was not mentioned in the Torah. Therefore it says <b>and Elohim finished</b> — meaning: He completed what had arisen in His will, may He be blessed, to make. And here He also removed the possibility that He performed creative work after the Sabbath in six other days. Understand this." "But one must pay attention to His saying <b>which He had made</b> — there was no need to say anything other than ״וַיְכַל אֱלֹהִים״ etc. ״His work.״ Further, one must note His saying <b>on the seventh day</b>, which implies that on the seventh day He performed creative work and completed it — but this is not so, for it was on the sixth day that He completed it. And although our Sages of blessed memory sensed this and sought to correct it, saying the following in their language (Genesis Rabbah 10:9): Rabbi Shimon says, flesh and blood does not know, etc., until here — with all this, the word ״on the day״ (בַּיּוֹם) is not precise, and according to their words the meaning is as though it were ״on the day.״ And in truth their words are words of truth, but there are yet more divine matters, and like a hammer it shatters [flashes]." "<b>And it appears</b> that the verse intends to speak in the manner of their saying, of blessed memory (Ḥagigah 12b), that the world was unstable and trembling until the Sabbath came and the world was established and firmly founded. And the intention is that on the day of Sabbath HaShem created the soul of the world, and this is the sod of His saying ״and on the seventh day He rested and was refreshed״ (וַיִּנָּפַשׁ). Although the Sages of blessed memory (Beitzah 16b) expounded ״since the Sabbath went forth וַיִּנָּפַשׁ״ — this is only the manner of derash that they derived from His saying ״וַיִּנָּפַשׁ״ and not saying ״וְנָפַשׁ״; they expounded that the verse intends to hint that since the Sabbath went forth, ״וַי״ — the soul was lost (נֶפֶשׁ / וַי). But the straightforward meaning of the verse does not depart from it, that it intends to say: since He rested — meaning, the Sabbath came — וַיִּנָּפַשׁ, meaning the vitality-bearing influence flowed into all created beings, for before the Sabbath there was no soul for any of the created beings." "And so we find in the words of our Sages of blessed memory in the holy book Zohar (Zohar, Tazria 44a) that they gave a reason for the commandment of HaShem to circumcise on the eighth day and not to bring an offering to HaShem until the eighth day — for the reason is that the Sabbath must first pass over him and he then becomes a possessor of soul. And this is what the verse says: <b>and Elohim finished</b> etc. — meaning that HaShem completed, in the midst of the seventh day, His work, and He went back and explained what His work that He completed was: not that anything was lacking from His making, regarding which He says ״וַיְכַל״, but the intention is ״which He had made״ — meaning, the work that He had already made was still lacking the thing that sustains it, and in the midst of the seventh day the matter was completed. And through this the difficulty we raised is resolved. Also through this we have succeeded in finding in the Torah the creation of the seventh day itself, in the manner that the Torah informed us of the creation of each day on its day, as it is written ״and it was evening״ etc. — this declares the coming-into-being of the evening and the coming-into-being of the morning; and here we do not find that He informed us of the creation of this day, but according to our approach it is indeed stated. And according to what I explained on the verse ״וַיְכֻלּוּ״ (Genesis 2:1) — that it is an expression of longing and desire — it can also be interpreted that His saying <b>and Elohim finished</b> etc. is in the manner of the verse (Job 14:15): ״for the work of Your hands You long״ (תִכְסֹף), and it is written (Deuteronomy 10:15): ״only in your fathers did He desire״ (חָשַׁק) — for the Master desired and took pleasure in His world, and this was in the midst of the seventh day when the world was established in the manner we explained: HaShem desired and was appeased by His creatures. And this matter was brought in their words (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 2), of blessed memory; and see nearby." "<b>And He rested on the seventh day</b>, etc. What does this verse come to teach us, since they are the very same things stated nearby: ״וַיְכַל אֱלֹהִים״?" "<b>And it appears</b> to say according to what they said, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 67; Pesachim 54a), that creatures were created on the eve of Sabbath with darkness, and they did not have time to complete the creation of their bodies before the day was sanctified, and these are the harmful beings — thus far. And we need to understand this: how can it be justified to say regarding the Creator, may His name be exalted, that He did not have time to complete, etc.? Is it not a thing known to all, that in the blink of an eye He can create all worlds. Go out and learn from what we wrote at the beginning of the parashah and you will see some of His mighty acts, may His name be exalted." "<b>Indeed</b>, the intention is that HaShem wished to make known the exceeding worth of the honored day. For you should know that the matter differs between work that one began and which would suffer no loss if left unfinished, and work that one has already begun and which would be spoiled if one does not complete it. And here Scripture informs us that not only did He not begin another work — but even with work that He had already begun, when the day was sanctified He rested from completing it as well, even though the work He had begun suffered a loss that admits of no repair; nevertheless He did not wish to do any more. And this is what He alluded to by saying <b>וַיִּשְׁבֹּת</b> after having said ״וַיְכַל״ — for not only did He finish and not add to make another act of creation, but even what was in His hand to complete, He rested from — meaning, He suspended completion of the matter that was in His hand. And therefore He said <b>מִכָּל מְלַאכְתּוֹ</b>, to say: even work that He had already begun. And this is His saying <b>אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה</b> — which He had begun — once the seventh day arrived He rested from completing it. And HaShem did so to demonstrate this matter, not on account of any lack of ability, Heaven forbid, for it is self-evident that in the blink of an eye He creates kohah and kohah, myriads upon myriads. I further say that for this reason HaShem arranged the works He did in the order of days, each day's matter in its day, in order that the worth of the Sabbath be recognized; for without this, HaShem could have performed the entire act of creation on a single day and in a brief time."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {1 "The closing phrase וּכְפַטִּישׁ יְפוֹצֵץ alludes to Jeremiah 23:29; translated hyper-literally.", 6 "כָּהֵנָּה וְכָהֵנָּה is an Aramaic idiom meaning 'this many and this many' (i.e., 'so many and so many'), preserved here in transliteration to convey the rhetorical flourish of the original; it does not refer to priests (kohanim)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:03.535413666Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}